= Global brain — Simple =

The '''global brain''' is a metaphor and research hypothesis that compares the worldwide network of people, organizations, computers, and communication systems with a brain.

The idea asks whether global networks can help humanity store knowledge, find useful information, coordinate action, and solve problems in ways that resemble some functions of a nervous system.

It does **not** establish that the Internet is literally a brain or that humanity has one shared consciousness.

== Historical ideas ==

Several older ideas contributed to the global-brain concept.

H. G. Wells proposed a continuously updated '''World Brain''' in the 1930s: a worldwide system for organizing and sharing reliable knowledge.<ref name="wells">[https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262542562/world-brain/ Wells, H. G., ''World Brain''.]</ref>

Pierre Teilhard de Chardin developed the [[Noosphere]], a philosophical idea about increasing planetary human thought and connection.

Later researchers used cybernetics and computer networks to study whether global communication could produce more effective distributed problem solving.

== The Internet as a possible substrate ==

The Internet and World Wide Web connect:

* people;
* documents;
* databases;
* organizations;
* software;
* artificial agents.

Search engines help route questions to information. Collaborative platforms combine contributions. Recommendation systems learn associations. Archives store external memory.

These functions make the brain analogy interesting.

But websites are not literally neurons and hyperlinks are not biological synapses.

== Collective intelligence ==

The most scientifically grounded part of the idea is [[Collective intelligence]].

Groups can sometimes solve tasks better than individuals by combining different knowledge or dividing work.

A planetary network could potentially make this easier by connecting more participants.

More connectivity is not automatically better. Networks can also spread:

* misinformation;
* manipulation;
* polarization;
* surveillance;
* correlated mistakes.

== Self-organization ==

Some global-brain theories emphasize self-organization.

Useful links can become stronger through repeated use. People can create new communities without one central planner. Systems can route information based on feedback.

Self-organization can produce useful order, but it can also produce undesirable outcomes. “Emergent” does not mean “good.”

== Global memory ==

Digital systems preserve huge amounts of information.

Libraries, repositories, archives, websites, and databases can act as external memory for society.

But information can disappear through link rot, censorship, platform shutdowns, inaccessible formats, or failed infrastructure.

Reliable global memory therefore needs preservation and governance.

== Power and control ==

A supposedly global network is not equally distributed.

Governments and large companies control important infrastructure, platforms, cloud systems, and algorithms.

This creates questions about:

* privacy;
* censorship;
* surveillance;
* unequal access;
* platform power.

A network can be technically distributed while decision power is centralized.

== Artificial intelligence ==

AI can help search, translate, summarize, plan, and coordinate.

That can make global networks more capable.

It can also make many people depend on the same few models. If the model is wrong, the same error can spread widely.

== What can actually be tested? ==

Researchers can test specific questions:

* Does adaptive search find experts faster?
* Does collaboration improve solution quality?
* Does network redundancy improve resilience?
* Which communication patterns preserve useful diversity?

These are scientific questions.

The stronger claim that the whole planet has become one mind requires separate evidence and has not been established.

